# scripts/redis/.env — targets for the read-only redis-triage MCP.
#
# Copy to scripts/redis/.env (git-ignored, and blocked by the .env-guard hook) and declare one
# variable per Redis you want to read. Nothing here is a credential — the access gate is your
# cloud IAM / network — but it is per-machine on purpose: a machine with no .env has no targets,
# which is the opt-in.
#
#   REDISPROD_<NAME>=<key>=<value>;<key>=<value>;…   → a PRODUCTION target, target="prod:<name>"
#   REDISSTG_<NAME>=<key>=<value>;<key>=<value>;…    → a STAGING target,    target="staging:<name>"
#
# The PREFIX decides the environment — same convention as PGPROD_/PGSTG_ in scripts/db/.env.
# REDISPROD_ ⇒ credential masking + PII provenance, and refused until this machine opts in
# (`triage: {prod: true}`). REDISSTG_ ⇒ values as-is, ungated. There is no `prod=` key: a line
# carrying one is reported by name and skipped, so a copied line can never silently downgrade a
# production box. <NAME> is UPPER_SNAKE; a bare `target="<name>"` works only when exactly one
# environment declares it.
#
# Keys:
#   host    (required)  address of the Redis as seen FROM the tunnel host (or from you, with
#                       tunnel=none) — a private IP or an internal DNS name
#   port                Redis port on that host (default 6379)
#   local   (required)  loopback port this process forwards to. Pick something that is NOT 6379:
#                       6379 is normally your LOCAL dev Redis, and shadowing it would answer a
#                       production question with dev data
#   tunnel              gcloud (default) ⇒ this server runs `gcloud compute ssh <vm>
#                                          --zone=<zone> -- -N -L <local>:<host>:<port>`
#                       none             ⇒ already reachable (your own bastion, a VPN, a
#                                          port-forward you manage); nothing is spawned
#   vm, zone            the compute instance to tunnel through (required for tunnel=gcloud)
#   project             gcloud project of that instance (default: your gcloud default)
#   iap                 true ⇒ add --tunnel-through-iap (default false)
#
# A forward you already run yourself on `local` is ADOPTED, not refused, when it is provably
# this target's: an ssh of yours, forwarding exactly local:host:port through `vm` (and the
# declared zone/project), and Redis answers PING through it. Anything else is refused and
# `scripts/redis/tunnel.sh status|kill` is named. An adopted forward is never stopped by the MCP.
#
# Migrating an existing env file: a staging line becomes REDISSTG_<NAME> and loses its
# `prod=false`; a production line keeps REDISPROD_<NAME> and drops `prod=true`. Until renamed,
# an old line is reported (by variable name) and skipped — never reinterpreted.
#
# Examples — replace with your own instances/addresses:

# A staging Redis, tunnelled through a staging VM. Values come back unmasked.
# REDISSTG_MAIN=host=10.0.0.10;port=6379;local=6378;tunnel=gcloud;vm=staging-vm;zone=your-zone

# The production Redis. Credentials are masked at the source and every value is fingerprinted
# into the PII provenance vault.
# REDISPROD_MAIN=host=10.0.0.20;port=6379;local=6377;tunnel=gcloud;vm=prod-redis-vm;zone=your-zone

# A Redis you can already reach (VPN, bastion, or a forward you run yourself).
# REDISPROD_MAIN=host=redis.internal.example.com;port=6379;local=6377;tunnel=none
